{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>You shall not do according to all that we do here today</b>. Rabbi Abraham (Ibn Ezra on Deuteronomy 12:8) said, according to the plain sense, that it was explained in it [this verse] because they were journeying from station to station, and one would offer a burnt offering at whatever station he pleased, and there is one who would give the firstborn of his flock, and there is one who would not give it, for the commandment depends on the Land. And this is not my opinion; rather, the reason is that not all of them were fearers of HaShem, and because He said <b>we</b>, for many commandments among the burnt offerings depend on the Land — this is his language. And it is not correct, for it is not the way of interpretation that He should speak in rebukes and say to them now that they do not fulfill the commandments of HaShem and each man does whatever is right in his own eyes — and how could Moses our teacher say, 'we do' in transgressions, Heaven forbid? Rather, it would have been fitting for Him to say, 'You shall not do according to all that you have done until this day, each man whatever is right in his own eyes,' and what reason would there be to include the commandments dependent on the Land together with the transgressions? But the explanation of the verse is that Israel were commanded in the wilderness to slaughter all their cattle and sheep as peace offerings before the Tabernacle (Leviticus 17:3–6), but at whatever place the Tabernacle happened to be, there they would offer them, and if one did not wish to eat an ox or a sheep he would not be obligated to bring an offering at all. And likewise he is not obligated to bring firstborns, nor the tithe of animals, nor the second tithe. And so he would not bring anything as an obligation to the Tabernacle at all, and even on the Festivals he was not obligated to come there. And likewise, after the throwing of the blood and the burning of the fat of the peace offerings, he may eat them in the wilderness wherever he pleases, for the verse did not assign them a boundary, but they eat them within the camp and outside the camp. And so they had no obligation at all regarding the sacrifices; rather, each man did whatever was right in his own eyes. Therefore He commanded here that after the rest and the inheritance they shall not do so, but they shall come as an obligation to a known and designated place chosen by HaShem, and they shall bring there the sacrifices and the tithes and the firstborns and eat them there within a boundary before HaShem. And what our Sages said (Sifrei 16) concerning the votive and freewill offering, which may be offered on a private altar, and that there is no votive or freewill offering that may not be offered on a private altar, is a midrash from the redundancy of the verse, as is explicit in the latter chapter of Zevaḥim (117a)."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {0 "The Sifrei citation refers to the halakhic midrash on Deuteronomy, parashah 16; the Talmudic reference is to Zevaḥim 117a in the final chapter."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:48:47.128010568Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}